{"name":"Gaboon Viper","hex":"#8c6450","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gaboon viper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Gaboon viper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gaboon_viper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gaboon viper","blocks":[{"text":"The Gaboon viper, also called the Gaboon adder, is a large and highly venomous species of viper found in the rainforests and savannas of sub-Saharan Africa. It is the largest member of the genus Bitis. Like all other vipers, it is venomous, and it has the longest fangs of any venomous snake – up to 2 inches (5.1 cm) in length – and the highest venom yield of any snake. No subspecies are recognized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Bitis_gabonica.jpg/330px-Bitis_gabonica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaboon_viper"},{"title":"Aucoumea klaineana","blocks":[{"text":"Aucoumea klaineana is a tree in the family Burseraceae, native to equatorial west Africa in Gabon, the Republic of the Congo, and Río Muni. It is a large hardwood tree growing to 30–40 m tall, rarely larger, with a trunk 1.0–2.5 m diameter above the often large basal buttresses. The tree generally grows in small stands, with the roots of the trees intertwined with neighboring trees. In Gabon, it is the primary timber species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/MPADN0021.jpg/330px-MPADN0021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aucoumea_klaineana"},{"title":"Viper","blocks":[{"text":"Vipers are snakes in the family Viperidae, found in most parts of the world, except for Antarctica, Australia, Hawaii, Madagascar, Ireland, and various other isolated islands. All vipers are venomous, and have long, hinged fangs that permit deep envenomation of their prey. Three subfamilies are currently recognized. They are also known as viperids. The name \"viper\" is derived from the Latin word vipera, -ae, also meaning viper, possibly from vivus (\"living\") and parere, referring to the trait viviparity common in vipers like most of the species of Boidae. The earliest known vipers are believed to have diverged from the rest of the clade Caenophidia in the early Eocene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Viperidae-01.jpg/330px-Viperidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viper"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png/500px-Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png/1280px-Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png/500px-Bitis_gabonica_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABitis_gabonica_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg/500px-Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg/1280px-Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg/500px-Bitis_gabonica_complete_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Complete skeleton 